  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Mostly cloudy skies are noted on\nsatellite this morning. Radar loop is showing isolated showers and\nthunderstorms across central Nevada this morning. The cells are\nmoving quickly at about 35 to 45 mph. Remote observation platforms\nare showing some precipitation with readings at or below a tenth\nof an inch at sites in White Pine county.\n\nFor today, upper level ridging continues to reside across Arizona\nand New Mexico with an upper trough over the Pacific Northwest at\nthis time. Moisture remains fairly high across northeast Nye, \nthrough White Pine and eastern Elko counties with precipitable \nwater values of 0.80 up to about an inch. Further west at other \nlocations, precipitable water values are at two-thirds to three- \nquarters of an inch. Another round of showers and thunderstorms \nare expected again today over most locations. Surface based CAPE \nvalues will generally run between 350-800J/kg. Lifted indices are \nforecast to be around -2C to -4C seen across east-central and \nnortheastern Nevada. In addition, 0-3km bulk shear values are \ncoming in fairly strong at 30 to 40 knots. DCAPE values across the\naforementioned area will be at or above 1000K/kg, which could \nlead to outflow wind gusts greater than 60 mph. Again, there is \nthe threat for heavy rain and its subsequent impacts if it falls \non recently burned locations, thus leading to debris flows and \nflash flooding. A marginal risk for excessive rainfall continues \nacross much of northern and central Nevada today and storms later \ntoday could lead to additional rain accumulations reaching half an\ninch or more for a two-day total. Highs today will be in the 70s \nand 80s with lows tonight in the 40s and 50s.\n\nThe high pressure center responsible for the recent subtropical\nmoisture push will slide slowly east tomorrow keeping residual \nmoisture restricted to the eastern edge of the state over the \nNevada and Utah borders. Due to the placement of the high to the \neast, the weather pattern will be dominated by the storm track \nover the western CONUS mainly to the north of the region. \nSeasonally average temperatures are forecast to continue into next\nweek.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continues for\nafternoon thunderstorm activity over the area today. Confidence \nis moderate regarding the areal coverage of precipitation today \nConfidence is low to moderate on the possibility of severe \nweather across central and northeast Nevada today as well. \nAdjustments were made to Wx, PoP grids in the short term to \nhighlight thunderstorm coverage and development.",
